WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.994
Clinical trials decide who can join with a long list of rules.

00:00:04.994 --> 00:00:09.416
Thirty, forty of them… written for doctors, not for patients.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:15.064
So here's a made-up patient.

00:00:15.064 --> 00:00:20.152
She's fifty, her breast cancer has spread… and she lives in Honolulu.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.740
She can travel up to five hundred kilometers.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:31.268
And none of this gets stored.

00:00:31.268 --> 00:00:33.458
It stays in her browser tab.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:40.955
Then a line in her own words: ER-positive, HER2-negative… no treatment yet.

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:44.487
Eighteen trials within five hundred kilometers.

00:00:44.487 --> 00:00:47.537
For seven of them, nothing likely rules her out.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:54.816
Each card adds it up: the rules she likely meets… and the ones only her doctor can answer.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:58.123
Let's open one.

00:00:58.123 --> 00:01:03.362
Every answer sits right next to the trial's own words, quoted exactly.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:11.991
This one's for ER-positive, HER2-negative breast cancer… and she likely meets it.

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:16.751
And when it doesn't know enough, it doesn't guess.

00:01:16.751 --> 00:01:20.652
It says: ask your doctor… and gives her the question to ask.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:28.158
She can print those questions and take them to her next appointment.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:36.717
Every trial links back to its official page.

00:01:36.717 --> 00:01:40.884
This is where the conversation starts… not where it ends.

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:44.759
TrialScout is a prototype.

00:01:44.759 --> 00:01:46.476
It's not medical advice.
